findings.body:
In the AccessTokenContext constructor, two consecutive Objects.requireNonNull calls both reference `grantType`:
```
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");
```
The second call should validate `rawTokenId`, not `grantType` again. As a result, a null `rawTokenId` is never rejected. If this constructor is ever invoked with a null raw id (directly, or via future callers of this public class), `encodeTokenId()` will silently concatenate the literal string "null" into the generated access-token id (e.g. `onrtac:null`) instead of failing fast, producing a malformed/non-unique token id (a potential jti collision across such tokens) instead of raising a clear error at construction time.

In `postInit()`, shortcuts obtained from `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory.getShortcut()` are inserted into `grantsByShortcuts`/`grantsToShortcuts` without any validation of length or null-ness:
```
String grantShortcut = gtf.getShortcut();
grantsByShortcuts.put(grantShortcut, grantName);
grantsToShortcuts.put(grantName, grantShortcut);
```
The downstream decoder (`DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.getTokenContextFromTokenId`) hard-codes an expectation of exactly 6 total characters (2+2+2) and will throw `IllegalArgumentException` for any other length, while `encodeTokenId()` has no such check and will happily build a malformed id for any shortcut length. A custom/third-party `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory` that returns a shortcut other than exactly 2 characters (which the companion Javadoc for `getShortcut()` even encourages by suggesting "3-letters") will successfully mint tokens with mis-sized ids that cannot be correctly decoded later, and a null shortcut will throw `NullPointerException` from the `ConcurrentHashMap.put` call during `postInit()`, aborting factory initialization.
